    "durationMs": 96739,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully verified. I built it with Express 5.3.0 + `pg` (PostgreSQL driver), and every requirement passed my manual HTTP tests:\n\n- **`POST /links`** — validates absolute http/https URLs with a host; returns `201 {code, url, clicks: 0}` with the URL unchanged; auto-generates 8-char codes with collision-retry; duplicate custom codes → `409`; missing/invalid url, invalid code (length and charset boundaries tested at 3/4/20/21), and malformed JSON all → `422`.\n- **`GET /links/{code}`** — returns the link or `404` for unknown codes.\n- **`GET /r/{code}`** — `302` with the correct `Location` header, atomically increments clicks exactly once per hit (`UPDATE ... SET clicks = clicks + 1 RETURNING`).\n- **`GET /links`** — ascending creation order via a serial `id`, with `total`.\n- **Persistence** — schema lives in `migrations/001_create_links.sql`; `scripts/migrate.js` tracks applied migrations in a `schema_migrations` table (skips them on re-run). I killed and restarted the server mid-test: all links and click counts survived, and `DATABASE_URL` is the only source of DB settings.\n- **`start.sh`** — applies pending migrations, then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; README documents how to start it.\n\nThe test server is stopped, so port 8000 is free for the real `sh start.sh` run."
  }
